# Copyright 2026 The Atlas Authors. Licensed under the Apache License 2.0.
#
# Installed to /etc/atlas/authority.conf, owned by root, mode 0644, on a
# root-owned path. See include/atlas/authority.h for the four conditions a grant
# requires; this file is the third of them.
#
# What it does: makes `atlas decision approve|reject|supersede|revalidate`
# possible for exactly one uid, and only when the running `atlas` executable is
# itself root-owned and unwritable by anyone else.
#
# What it does not do: identify a person. `operator_uid` names an OS principal.
# A terminal, a pseudo-terminal, a typed confirmation and an environment
# variable are all producible by any process running as that uid, and none of
# them is treated as evidence that a human acted. `LOCAL_OPERATOR_CONFIRMED`
# records which channel was used, never who used it.

# The uid permitted to run a lifecycle transition.
#
# In the A7.1 deployment this is **the `atlasd` uid**, not the operator's login
# account — because `atlasd` is the only principal that can open the database at
# all, and an authority grant to an account that cannot reach the index would
# authorise nothing. The human workflow is therefore deliberately offline and
# deliberately awkward:
#
#   1. a separate trusted terminal, not one an AI process controls
#   2. stop the service through root authentication
#   3. run the root-owned binary as atlasd against the authoritative index
#   4. perform exactly one lifecycle operation
#   5. start the service, verify the ledger, invalidate the sudo timestamp
#
# `docs/security/A7_1_OPERATIONS.md` has the exact commands. That workflow is
# not exposed through MCP, the plugin, or any model-callable helper, and must
# not be.
#
#   operator_uid = <atlasd uid>
